  "summary_md": "Today’s batch across Computer Hardware and Medical Care Facilities is marked by a wave of strategic repositioning and M&A, as companies pivot to AI, defense, and GLP‑1‑driven care while others exit entirely. NNDM is abandoning additive manufacturing for an unproven AI‑diagnostics merger, UMAC is capitalizing on a federal drone‑component ban with potential direct Pentagon funding, and WW is gaining clinical‑weight‑loss traction even as legacy behavioral subscriptions erode. Buyout activity is concentrated: EHAB has drawn competing bids above an existing take‑private deal, ALOT accepted a premium all‑cash offer that crystallized a turnaround, and AMS remains in extended talks after rejecting earlier overtures.\n\nAlongside these transformations, hardware names are reframing around recurring revenue (TACT, CRCT) and AI infrastructure (CRSR, QMCO post‑debt elimination), while medical providers navigate uneven demand and margin recovery. Fragility persists where cash‑burn and dilution overhang clash with growth narratives—UAVS, VELO, and AMCI face deep skepticism despite defense or robotics catalysts—while AMN’s staffing rebound remains tentative. The batch captures an inflection‑heavy moment where capital discipline and the credibility of strategic leaps will determine which stories hold.",
